"use strict";(globalThis.webpackChunkmariag=globalThis.webpackChunkmariag||[]).push([[346],{4332:e=>{e.exports=JSON.parse('{"archive":{"blogPosts":[{"id":"/designing-docs-for-restraint","metadata":{"permalink":"/portfolio/blog/designing-docs-for-restraint","source":"@site/blog/designing-docs-for-restraint.mdx","title":"I rebuilt my docs site to be boring on purpose","description":"I spent a while arguing that technical documentation could carry gradients and glassmorphism. Then I rebuilt this site without them, and here is what actually survived the rewrite.","date":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Design","permalink":"/portfolio/blog/tags/docs-design","description":"Visual systems, gradients, and UX writing craft."},{"inline":false,"label":"Accessibility","permalink":"/portfolio/blog/tags/accessibility","description":"Ensuring colorful docs stay readable and inclusive."},{"inline":false,"label":"UX writing","permalink":"/portfolio/blog/tags/ux-writing","description":"Microcopy, structure, and task flows that respect the reader."}],"readingTime":2.57,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Maria Gilca","title":"Technical writer & information architect","url":"https://mariagilca.github.io/portfolio/about/","email":"mariag@voix.com","socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/maria-gilca/","github":"https://github.com/mariagilca"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/mariagilca.png","key":"mariag","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"I rebuilt my docs site to be boring on purpose","description":"I spent a while arguing that technical documentation could carry gradients and glassmorphism. Then I rebuilt this site without them, and here is what actually survived the rewrite.","date":"2026-08-03T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["design","accessibility","ux-writing"],"authors":"mariag","keywords":["documentation design","technical documentation typography","accessibility","docs design system"],"image":"/img/social-card.png"},"unlisted":false,"nextItem":{"title":"Treating Docusaurus like a docs-as-code platform","permalink":"/portfolio/blog/docusaurus-docs-as-code"}},"content":"For a while I argued that technical documentation could carry a bit of\\natmosphere (nebula gradients, glass panels, motion on scroll) as long as you\\nkept the contrast honest. I built this site that way, and I don\'t think the\\nargument was wrong exactly. It was just answering a question nobody had asked.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\nThen I rebuilt the site with almost none of it. Below is what survived, because\\nthose parts turned out not to be about decoration at all.\\n\\n## Structure first, and it turns out that was the whole thing\\n\\nThe original version of this advice was \\"break pages into predictable sections,\\n*then* layer gradients behind them.\\" The first half was doing all the work. Once\\nthe hierarchy is genuinely obvious, the gradient adds nothing a reader needs; and\\nif the hierarchy isn\'t obvious, no amount of atmosphere rescues it.\\n\\nWhat replaced the gradients was spacing and one typographic rule: headings in one\\nfamily, running prose in another, metadata in a third, and never mix them up. On\\nthis site sans is the interface voice, a serif carries every piece of prose, and\\nmono is reserved for metadata: owner, date, term ID. A reader can now tell what\\n*kind* of thing they\'re looking at before they read a word of it. That\'s the job\\nI had been trying to give a background.\\n\\n## Transparency was never the accessible option\\n\\nThe old post said glassmorphism in docs is \\"tricky\\" because we render code\\nblocks, tables and diagrams over it, and recommended compensating with thicker\\nborders and drop shadows.\\n\\nRead that back and it\'s a description of fighting your own design. Every\\ncompensating detail (heavier borders, stacked shadows, raised text\\ncontrast) existed to undo a problem transparency introduced. Solid surfaces and a single\\nhairline border cost nothing and need no compensation. Contrast ratios stopped\\nbeing something I had to check defensively and started being a property of the\\npalette.\\n\\n## Performance is a content decision\\n\\nThe version of this site you\'re reading loads no web fonts. Not because\\nself-hosting is hard, but because I sell documentation systems for teams who care\\nwhether their docs are fast, and a portfolio that spends 200kB on typefaces is\\nmaking an argument against its author. The platform stacks render well, and the\\nbudget went to things a reader can actually use.\\n\\nThe animated background went for the same reason. It was the most-praised thing\\non the old site and the least useful: it competed with first paint on exactly the\\npage where someone decides whether to keep reading.\\n\\n## Document the design system like any other feature\\n\\nThis one I\'d keep verbatim. Examples, dos and don\'ts, and token references belong\\nin the repo, so a future contributor stays consistent without asking a designer.\\nThe only change is that there is far less to document now, which is its own kind\\nof win, and roughly the point.\\n\\n---\\n\\nThe honest summary: I was solving for how the site felt to build rather than how\\nit felt to read. Those aren\'t the same problem, and only one of them is the\\nreader\'s."},{"id":"/docusaurus-docs-as-code","metadata":{"permalink":"/portfolio/blog/docusaurus-docs-as-code","source":"@site/blog/docusaurus-docs-as-code.mdx","title":"Treating Docusaurus like a docs-as-code platform","description":"How I wire branching, review gates and CI into a Docusaurus repo so documentation ships on the product\'s schedule instead of after it.","date":"2025-11-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":[{"inline":false,"label":"Docusaurus","permalink":"/portfolio/blog/tags/docusaurus","description":"Tutorials and patterns for building with Docusaurus v3."},{"inline":false,"label":"Docs-as-code","permalink":"/portfolio/blog/tags/docs-as-code","description":"Workflows that treat docs like production software."},{"inline":false,"label":"CI/CD","permalink":"/portfolio/blog/tags/ci-cd","description":"Pipelines, preview builds, and release automation for documentation."}],"readingTime":1.55,"hasTruncateMarker":true,"authors":[{"name":"Maria Gilca","title":"Technical writer & information architect","url":"https://mariagilca.github.io/portfolio/about/","email":"mariag@voix.com","socials":{"linkedin":"https://www.linkedin.com/in/maria-gilca/","github":"https://github.com/mariagilca"},"imageURL":"https://github.com/mariagilca.png","key":"mariag","page":null}],"frontMatter":{"title":"Treating Docusaurus like a docs-as-code platform","description":"How I wire branching, review gates and CI into a Docusaurus repo so documentation ships on the product\'s schedule instead of after it.","date":"2025-11-20T00:00:00.000Z","tags":["docusaurus","docs-as-code","ci/cd"],"authors":"mariag","keywords":["docs as code","Docusaurus CI/CD","documentation pipeline","technical writing workflow"],"image":"/img/social-card.png"},"unlisted":false,"prevItem":{"title":"I rebuilt my docs site to be boring on purpose","permalink":"/portfolio/blog/designing-docs-for-restraint"}},"content":"## Align docs velocity with product velocity\\n\\nDocs-as-code succeeds only when writers share the same branching rituals as\\nengineers. Mirror the product repo: one `main` branch, short-lived feature\\nbranches, and required status checks. Docusaurus plays nicely when you split\\nconfiguration (`docusaurus.config.js`, `sidebars.js`) from content, so diffs stay\\nreadable and a reviewer can see what actually changed.\\n\\n\x3c!-- truncate --\x3e\\n\\n## Keep builds fast and opinionated\\n\\nAdd CI jobs for linting MDX, validating links, and generating preview deploys with\\n`docusaurus build`. Cache `node_modules` and the `.docusaurus` output so builds\\nfeel instant: a fast signal keeps reviewers engaged, and a slow one teaches\\npeople to approve without looking.\\n\\nTwo settings are worth making non-negotiable, because both only take effect in\\nproduction builds and will otherwise fail silently in local development:\\n\\n```js title=\\"docusaurus.config.js\\"\\nonBrokenLinks: \'throw\',\\nmarkdown: {\\n  hooks: {onBrokenMarkdownLinks: \'throw\'},\\n},\\n```\\n\\nIf a broken internal link can\'t fail the build, it will reach production. One\\ncaveat if your sitemap uses `lastmod`: it is resolved from git history, so a CI\\njob checking out with the default shallow clone silently drops it. `fetch-depth: 0`.\\n\\n## Make visual QA effortless\\n\\nWriters should not have to guess how a component renders in production. Generate\\npreview URLs for every branch, and keep a page in the repo that renders each\\nshared component with real content, so the team updates it once and every page\\ninherits the change.\\n\\n## Capture tribal knowledge in the repo\\n\\nCI secrets, content strategy decisions, voice and tone guidance, launch\\nchecklists: all of it belongs next to the content it governs. Use admonitions\\n(`:::tip`) to mark the things someone will otherwise have to ask about, and\\ninclude runnable snippets so a future contributor can extend your work without\\nmessaging you.\\n\\nThe test of docs-as-code isn\'t whether the pipeline is elegant. It\'s whether\\ndocumentation ships in the same release as the feature it describes."}]}}')}}]);